rt1 patch 0007 --- [ARM] Let a musttail vararg forwarder that calls va_start be tail-called

Upstream commit in the rt1 tree: a20eeb750.  Closes catalog item #6.

WHAT THE VETO ACTUALLY WAS --- and what the recon got wrong about it.

  ARMISelLowering.cpp:3086-3091 in the unpatched tree reads

      // If Caller's vararg or byval argument has been split between registers and
      // stack, do not perform tail call, since part of the argument is in caller's
      // local frame.
      const ARMFunctionInfo *AFI_Caller = MF.getInfo<ARMFunctionInfo>();
      if (AFI_Caller->getArgRegsSaveSize())
        return false;

  scratch/T8/RECON-issue6.md argued in part from "the guard's own comment describes
  a different hazard (byval split)".  That pillar is STRUCK: the comment names
  vararg FIRST, and upstream main narrowed the same line to `CLI.IsVarArg &&
  AFI_Caller->getArgRegsSaveSize()` --- i.e. upstream reads this guard as PRIMARILY
  about varargs (upstream-check/ARMISelLowering.main.cpp:2877-2884, banked).  That
  narrowing does not fix #6: a musttail vararg forwarder's call site is variadic by
  construction, so CLI.IsVarArg is true and the veto still fires.  main:2765-2769
  now documents the consequence in its own words --- a false return here on a valid
  musttail call is a fatal backend error --- so upstream knows and has not fixed it.

  The recon's VERDICT (conservative guard, small patch) survives on the other two
  lines of evidence, and this patch rests on those.

SCOPE --- musttail only, and not Thumb1.

  Same MCAsmInfo::usesWindowsCFI() reasoning as patch 0003: the SEH path is taken by
  BOTH ARMCOFFMCAsmInfoMicrosoft (thumbv7-*-windows-msvc) and ARMCOFFMCAsmInfoGNU
  (MinGW ARM32).  rt1 validates on MSVC only; MinGW is in-scope-by-construction, NOT
  tested.  The three lit RUN lines cover Thumb2/COFF, Thumb2/ELF and ARM mode.

  Thumb1 (including v8m.baseline, which supportsTailCall() allows) is deliberately
  left on the old path: there the save area is released by
  Thumb1FrameLowering::emitPopSpecialFixUp, which is written around a tBX_RET /
  tPOP_RET terminator and not around a TCRETURN.  A v8m.baseline forwarder still
  reports the fatal error rather than taking an untested epilogue.  This patch
  touches no frame lowering at all.

WHAT THE LIT DIRECTIVES DO --- discriminating vs pinning, stated honestly.

  39 directives total: 27 CHECK (run three times, once per triple), 11 MIR, 1 FATAL
  (used by two RUN lines).  MEASURED against three compilers this session:

    * vs the UNPATCHED compiler (rt1 345ce564e / stock 18.1.8): all 38 asm+MIR
      directives fail --- but for ONE cause, because llc aborts on @fwd_vastart
      before emitting anything.  Count that as ONE discriminating signal, not 38.

    * vs MUTANT B (the veto loosened for every call, not only musttail): the test
      FAILS at line 110, `; CHECK: bl plain` in @tail_vastart.  That is the single
      measured directive that discriminates the SCOPE of the change --- it is the
      V2 control.  @tail_novastart directly above it is the armed floor: same
      signature minus va_start, and it does emit a branch, so the directive class
      demonstrably reads both ways.

    * vs MUTANT A (the new byval veto deleted): the test still PASSES.  So every
      byval-related directive in this file, including the FATAL line, is a PIN and
      not a control.  The reason is worth recording: a byval argument reaches
      MatchingStackOffset (ARMISelLowering.cpp:2984-2998) as a FrameIndex, matches
      neither the CopyFromReg nor the LoadSDNode case, and falls through to the
      final `return false`.  So musttail-plus-byval is already refused there.

  The new ByValArgRegsSaveSize guard is therefore DEFENCE IN DEPTH, not a
  load-bearing decision today, and it is kept for that reason and no other: without
  it the safety of musttail-plus-byval would rest entirely on a fallthrough in an
  unrelated helper.  ByValArgRegsSaveSize <= ArgRegsSaveSize always (the va_start
  block can only lower ArgRegBegin), so for every non-musttail call the two new
  conditions are exactly equivalent to the single old one.

BLAST RADIUS --- measured, not asserted.

  A/B over llvm/test/CodeGen/{ARM,Thumb,Thumb2}/*.ll --- 2,045 files x 2 triples
  (thumbv7-unknown-windows-msvc, armv7-unknown-linux-gnueabi) = 4,090 pairs ---
  comparing llc built at 345ce564e against llc built with this patch:

      compiled and byte-compared : 3,685
      skipped (BOTH arms failed identically, same exit code) :   405
      DIFFERING                  :     0

  BYTE-IDENTICAL, and no pair differed even in exit code.  (Five of the skipped
  pairs segfault BOTH arms identically; that is pre-existing and unrelated.)

  The identity arm is deliberately NOT tools/ab_arm.sh: both arms are built from
  the SAME commit, so --version is identical by construction and ab_arm_two()
  would correctly refuse them.  That sameness is the point --- it removes the
  producer-string confound (the +188 B mechanism that contaminated the Bug-4
  draft) entirely, since the only difference between the two binaries is this
  patch.  The arm is instead (a) SHA-256 inequality --- 7497b4bc... vs f47cea50...
  --- and (b) a behavioural control run before anything is compared: the baseline
  arm MUST fail on the banked #6 repro (measured rc=3) and the patched arm MUST
  succeed (measured rc=0).  The comparator itself is armed by injecting a byte
  into one output and requiring it to be reported; it was.
  Harness: scratch/T8/w/ab_corpus.sh (bucket LIVE).

  Independently, the lit sweep over CodeGen/{ARM,Thumb,Thumb2} + DebugInfo/COFF
  is unchanged: 2,468 discovered, the same 24 failing before and after, every one
  of them exit-127 because llvm-pdbutil / llvm-readelf / llvm-dwarfdump / obj2yaml
  are not built in this configuration.  No FileCheck mismatch anywhere.

WHAT THIS PATCH DOES NOT ESTABLISH.

  It is validated by lit, by the A/B above, and by the post-PEI MIR arithmetic
  pinned in the test.  There is NO fleet consumer: Varan emits no musttail +
  va_start shape, so unlike 0001-0006 this one has never run on a device.  The
  runtime executable required by scratch/T8/ACCEPTANCE-0007.md V4 --- reading values
  back through a forwarded va_list against x64-derived expectations --- is NOT part
  of this patch and is the outstanding acceptance item.

